Alright, I’ve just added the motion keyframes (`rise`, `glow-drift`, `grid-drift`, etc.) and hooked the background grid animation into the appropriate CSS selector. The next logical step is to actually apply the `rise` animation to the hero section’s textual elements so they fade in and slide up as the page loads. 

Looking through the HTML file, I see a `.hero-eyebrow` rule that already defines the eyebrow style. It’s a good anchor point because it’s the first selector in the hero block. If I extend that block to include the other hero selectors (`.hero h1`, `.hero-content > p`, `.hero-stats`) and add the `opacity: 0` base plus the `animation: rise … forwards` declaration, I’ll get the desired entrance effect. I also need to stagger the animation with appropriate delays for each element, matching the pattern I used earlier for other components.

I’ll locate the exact text of the existing `.hero-eyebrow` rule to replace it. The current block is:

```
.hero-eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.3em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #a8926a;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
}
```

I’ll keep that unchanged, then append the new CSS after the closing brace. The new snippet should be:

```
.hero-eyebrow,
.hero h1,
.hero-content > p,
.hero-stats {
    opacity: 0;
    animation: rise 0.9s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) forwards;
}

.hero h1           { animation-delay: 0.12s; }
.hero-content > p  { animation-delay: 0.26s; }
.hero-stats        { animation-delay: 0.42s; }
```

That way the eyebrow itself also participates in the fade‑in, and the main heading, paragraph, and stats get progressively delayed, creating a smooth cascade.

Since I only want to modify this specific block and not replace any other occurrences, I’ll set `replace_all` to false. The edit operation will replace the old `.hero-eyebrow` block with the combined old block plus the new rules. This should integrate cleanly with the rest of the stylesheet and trigger the motion I just defined.

